---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Analog Synthesizer">

  <section class="prose max-w-none">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Analog Synthesizer</h1>

    <!-- Project Description -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Project Description</h2>

    <p>
      This project is a fully analog synthesizer with a digital waveform display.
      The audio signal path is constructed from discrete components, op-amps, and
      operational transconductance amplifiers (OTAs). The synthesizer includes a
      function generator capable of producing square, triangle, and sine waves
      from 16 Hz to 16 kHz, followed by a state-variable filter,
      voltage-controlled amplifier, and automatic gain control stage.
    </p>

    <p>
      The major voltage-controlled stages can be modulated using control signals
      generated by low-frequency oscillators operating from 0.1 Hz to 10 Hz and
      ADSR envelope generators. Exponential converters can also be used to shape
      the control voltages and provide nonlinear modulation characteristics.
    </p>

    <p>
      The final audio signal is sent through an output amplifier and speaker.
      Alongside the analog circuitry, an ESP32 samples control voltages and audio
      signals and displays their waveforms on an LCD, providing a digital
      visualization of the synthesizer's operation.
    </p>

    <!-- Block Diagram -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Block Diagram</h2>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/block3.png"
        alt="Block diagram of the analog synthesizer"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The block diagram shows the overall architecture of the synthesizer and
      the flow of both audio signals and control voltages. The primary audio path
      begins with waveform generation in the function generator, followed by
      filtering, amplitude control, automatic gain control, and final audio
      amplification.
    </p>

    <p>
      Supporting control circuitry provides modulation signals to the
      voltage-controlled stages. These control signals can be produced using
      low-frequency oscillators or ADSR envelope generators and can optionally
      be passed through exponential converters before reaching the controlled
      circuit.
    </p>

    <p>
      The diagram also shows the digital subsystem used to sample and display
      signals on the LCD, as well as the power-management circuitry that
      supplies the analog and digital sections of the system.
    </p>

    <!-- Schematic -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Schematics</h2>

    <p>
      The following schematics show several of the major analog subsystems used
      throughout the synthesizer. These include the waveform generator,
      state-variable filter, voltage-controlled amplifier, automatic gain
      control circuit, and control-voltage generator.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/FG.png"
        alt="Function generator schematic"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/SVF.png"
        alt="State-variable filter schematic"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/VCA.png"
        alt="Voltage-controlled amplifier schematic"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/AGC.png"
        alt="Automatic gain control schematic"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/CVG.png"
        alt="Control-voltage generator schematic"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The analog sections are designed so that important parameters can be
      adjusted manually or controlled using generated control voltages.
      Potentiometers provide direct control over parameters such as waveform
      frequency, filter cutoff, and signal amplitude, while the modulation
      circuitry allows those same parameters to vary automatically over time.
    </p>

    <p>
      This architecture allows the synthesizer to operate as a manually
      controlled instrument while also supporting dynamic modulation through
      internally generated control signals. A separate manual volume control
      sets the final output level before the signal reaches the speaker
      amplifier.
    </p>

    <!-- Next Steps -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Next Steps</h2>

    <p>
      The next stage of the project is to continue simulating and verifying the
      individual subsystems in LTSpice against their design targets. Once the
      circuits are fully validated, the complete PCB will be laid out,
      fabricated, assembled, and physically tested.
    </p>

    <p>
      A custom enclosure will then be modeled and 3D printed, and the ESP32
      firmware responsible for sampling signals and rendering waveforms on the
      LCD will be developed and integrated with the completed hardware.
    </p>

    <style>
      .project-figure {
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
      }

      .project-image {
        width: 100%;
        display: block;
        border-radius: 8px;
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
        background: #f5f5f5;
      }

      .project-figure figcaption {
        margin-top: 0.5rem;
        text-align: center;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        opacity: 0.7;
      }
    </style>

  </section>

</BaseLayout>